The board position has no immediate winning move for either player (verified by exhaustive enumeration of the seven legal placements for \(o\) and for \(x\)). Both sides also lack any ready three-in-a-row whose fourth cell is currently playable, so neither side can create a forced threat on the present turn.

The only living lines that already contain two of \(o\)’s discs are slow or blocked. Consequently the short-term strategic goal is to create at least one living pair whose two possible extensions are both free; the player who first obtains such a configuration can force a threat on the subsequent move and thereby seize the initiative.

The unique cell that simultaneously realises three distinct living pairs is \((2,4)\):

- vertical pair with the existing disc at \((3,4)\) (extensions \((1,4)\) and \((0,4)\));
- diagonal pair with the existing disc at \((3,5)\) (extensions \((1,3)\) and \((4,6)\));
- horizontal pair with the existing disc at \((2,3)\) (the only extension that is not immediately poisoned by \(x\)’s own three-in-a-row).

All three of the indicated extension cells are free after the placement. Moreover the two most useful pairs (vertical and diagonal) have completely disjoint sets of extension cells. Therefore, after \(o\) occupies column 4, \(x\) can destroy at most one of the pairs; \(o\) can still convert the surviving pair into a concrete threat on the next move and retain the initiative.

No other legal cell produces a comparable collection of living, immediately convertible pairs. In particular:

- column 6 yields only two pairs and leaves the high-value intersection \((2,4)\) available for \(x\);
- column 2 opens the cell \((3,2)\) from which \(x\) can immediately threaten his already-completed diagonal;
- columns 0, 1, 3 and 5 produce at most a single (often dead) pair.

Hence the unique optimal opening move is column 4.

Final Answer: 4

